  ["R1-12", "The novelty discussion does not identify a precise contribution beyond combining known adaptive mechanisms, and does not compare decision rules, information requirements, or costs directly against AHIBCS and FedFairCS.", "We can and do tighten the textual novelty framing to avoid overstating distinctiveness (reflected in the corrected Conclusion, Replacement 18). A rigorous head-to-head comparison of decision rules, information requirements, and measured computational cost against AHIBCS/FedFairCS under one common framework is an experimental undertaking we cannot perform without the original implementation; this is disclosed as future work (Replacement 22, item 5).", "disclosed"],
  ["R1-13", "The two-variant ablation (A1, A2) does not isolate KL utility, the fairness term, the uncertainty penalty, the controller, or the baseline-derived reference individually.", "The ablation discussion is corrected to state precisely what A1/A2 vary (cohort sizing and aggregation jointly) and what they do not isolate (Replacement 13). Targeted single-component ablations require new training runs and are disclosed as not performed (Replacement 22, item 2).", "disclosed"],
  ["R1-14", "Baseline comparisons need clearer provenance (reproduced vs. copied from source papers) and consistent naming (Pow-D vs. FedProx in Configuration B).", "Replacement 14 states exactly which methods are compared in each configuration and their stated hyperparameters; the Pow-D/FedProx naming inconsistency is corrected throughout (Replacement 11/14). Whether every individual baseline number was reproduced under this paper's own implementation versus taken from its source publication is not something we can state with certainty without the original experiment logs; this is disclosed rather than asserted either way (Replacement 22, item 5).", "disclosed"],
  ["R1-15", "No independent runs or uncertainty estimates are reported; it is unclear whether reported accuracy is final-round, best-observed, or averaged.", "The evaluation-endpoint protocol is now stated precisely (round-to-target on a defined split; final vs. peak vs. checkpoint accuracy are distinguished and not interchanged; unattained targets are recorded as \u201cnot reached\u201d) (Replacement 12). Multi-seed statistical reporting requires new training runs, which we cannot perform without the recovered code/data; this is disclosed explicitly as not yet done (Replacement 22, item 1).", "disclosed"],
  ["R1-16", "Implementation details are missing: ResNet-18 pretraining checkpoint, preprocessing, grayscale handling, batch-norm treatment, and hyperparameter initialization in every configuration.", "We can state the dataset-specific output-head sizes precisely (Replacement 18). The remaining low-level preprocessing/checkpoint details were not recorded in the materials available to us after the loss described above; rather than reconstruct them from memory, we disclose this as missing information to be supplied before the next revision if the original configuration can be recovered (Replacement 22, item 8).", "disclosed"],
  ["R1-17", "The COVID-19 dataset is described as 3-class, but the model description states a 10-class output head.", "The model description is corrected to specify a 3-class head for COVID-19 and a 10-class head for MNIST/FMNIST/CIFAR-10 (Replacement 18). Patient-level grouping, duplicate checks, and recall/F1 averaging convention for this dataset cannot be confirmed without the original data split; disclosed accordingly (Replacement 22, item 6).", "disclosed"],
  ["R1-18", "Configuration B participation counts (e.g., COVID-19: 1047/103\u224810.17, exceeding the stated cap of 10) need reconciliation with disclosed per-configuration bounds and per-round logs.", "The cohort-sizing text now states explicitly that the Configuration A bounds (8, 10) are not asserted to apply to Configuration B, and asks that configuration-specific bounds be disclosed (Replacement 7). Per-round cohort logs for Configuration B are not available in the recovered materials; disclosed as missing rather than reconciled by assumption (Replacement 22, item 8).", "disclosed"],
  ["R1-19", "The results discussion overstates trade-offs — e.g., FedFACS takes more rounds than AHIBCS on FMNIST for a small upload reduction, DivFL is faster on MNIST, and FedFACS does not win every Table 6 condition.", "Replacement 11 rewrites the Configuration B results discussion to state rounds-vs-uploads trade-offs plainly in both directions (more rounds, fewer uploads) rather than as unqualified improvement.", "fixed"],
  ["R1-20", "Specific reporting inconsistencies: FMNIST-S2 accuracy 83.80% (Table 1) vs. 83.08% (text); Fig. 9 mislabels percentage-point gains as relative percentages; ambiguity about whether 200-round entries mean target attainment or budget exhaustion.", "All three issues are corrected: the 83.80% figure is used consistently (Replacement 11); relative vs. absolute-point gains are now distinguished explicitly (Replacement 15); and the evaluation-endpoint protocol records \u201cnot reached\u201d outcomes instead of treating budget exhaustion as success (Replacement 12).", "fixed"],
  ["R1-21", "The algorithms should be rewritten as one consistent, executable account (reset timing, when scores are computed, when counters update), and a reproducibility package is requested.", "Algorithm 1's heading, inputs, and loss term are corrected (Replacement 17); the post-aggregation validation/state-commitment sequence is clarified with explicit staging and commit semantics (Replacement 9). A full reproducibility package (configs, partitions, seeds, per-round scores, cohort selections, aggregation weights) cannot be produced without the original code and data; this is disclosed as outstanding (Replacement 22, item 8).", "disclosed"],
];
