\subsubsection{Configuration A: comparison with FedAvg, FedProx, Pow-D, DivFL and Zhai et al.}
Targets follow \citet{zhai2024}: 44\%, 48\% and 44\% on CIFAR-10, 97\%, 98\% and 97\% on MNIST, and 84\%, 87\% and 80\% on FMNIST for S1 ($\alpha=0.1$), S1 ($\alpha=0.5$) and S2, respectively; the FedFACS columns of Tables~\ref{tab:abl-rounds} and~\ref{tab:rounds} coincide, so the ablation uses the same targets. Table~\ref{tab:rounds} reports the rounds required. Relative to Zhai et al., FedFACS needs fewer rounds in all nine settings: by 9.1\%, 8.1\% and 2.7\% on CIFAR-10, by 14.7\%, 5.5\% and 0.9\% on MNIST and by 4.7\%, 2.0\% and 2.8\% on FMNIST, computed from Table~\ref{tab:rounds}. The reduction is largest where target accuracy is reached early and smallest under the strongest skew. DivFL reaches the MNIST targets much faster under both S1 settings (28 and 35 rounds versus 58 and 69), and FedFACS trades this speed for higher final accuracy than DivFL: the original evaluation reports differences of 1.19, 0.74 and 1.98 on MNIST (it does not state whether these are absolute or relative). Entries equal to 200 equal the training budget; the original evaluation does not distinguish reaching a target in round 200 from not reaching it, so these entries should be read as ``not reached, or reached only at the budget limit''. FedFACS final accuracies are 47.07, 51.12 and 47.33 on CIFAR-10, 98.52, 98.91 and 98.11 on MNIST and 86.63, 88.95 and 83.80 on FMNIST (Table~\ref{tab:abl-acc}); the original evaluation reports differences over the best baseline of 0.48, 0.89 and 0.67 (CIFAR-10), 0.27, 0.31 and 0.22 (MNIST), and $+0.35$, $+0.85$ and $-0.2$ (FMNIST), again without stating absolute or relative units. On FMNIST S2 FedFACS is therefore marginally below the best baseline in accuracy. Cumulative aggregated clients (Fig.~\ref{fig:cum}) are lower than those of the best-performing baseline by roughly 4--12.5\% (CIFAR-10), 7--17\% (MNIST) and 2.3--5.8\% (FMNIST) according to the original evaluation.

