\subsubsection{Discussion}
Across the evaluated settings, the evidence supports a trade-off rather than uniform superiority. FedFACS reduced the number of rounds relative to Zhai et al.\ in every Configuration~A setting and gave modest accuracy gains in most, but it was slower than DivFL on MNIST. Relative to AHIBCS it raised accuracy and reduced cumulative uploads in all three Configuration~B datasets at the cost of more rounds on FMNIST and CIFAR-10 and lower COVID-19 recall. In Configuration C it matched or exceeded FedFairCS on JFI in five of six settings but not in all. The conditions under which the trade-off is advantageous are those in which upload volume and fairness weigh more than the extra rounds or the extra download and local-training cost borne by non-selected candidates; whether that holds for a given deployment depends on a communication and compute model that we did not measure.

%% ------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limits}
The results have the following scope and limitations.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Correspondence between specification and results.} The results were produced before the formulation was corrected and could not be regenerated (Section~\ref{sec:scope}); the corrected specification has not been evaluated.
\item \textbf{Statistical evidence.} Every value comes from a single run, with no standard deviations or confidence intervals. Differences of one to three rounds, under one accuracy point or of 0.006 in JFI should be read as descriptive. A rigorous comparison needs at least five independently seeded runs per method with matched partitions and paired confidence intervals.
\item \textbf{Ablation.} FedFACS-A1/A2 vary cohort sizing and aggregation jointly. The individual contributions of the KL utility, fairness term, variance penalty and controller are not isolated, and the gains cannot be attributed to a specific component.
\item \textbf{Validity of the utility and the penalty.} We did not test whether $\KL_i^t$ correlates with the held-out loss change produced by incorporating a client's update, or whether the variance penalty separates corrupted from heterogeneous-but-useful updates. Sensitivity to $B$, $\mu$, $\beta$, $\lambda$, the batch size and the cohort bounds was not studied.
\item \textbf{Controller design.} The scale mismatch between $F_i^t\ge1$ and $\KL_i^t$ (Section~\ref{sec4.4}) is not corrected, and no stability, convergence or fairness guarantee is established.
\item \textbf{Communication and computation.} Only cumulative uploads were counted. All candidates download the model, train and score each round, and the corresponding downlink bytes, training steps, scoring passes and wall-clock time were not measured; the accounting of Eq.~\eqref{eq:btotal} is a definition, not a result.
\item \textbf{Fairness evidence.} JFI is quality-adjusted. Selection-frequency distributions, maximum waiting times, cumulative aggregation influence, the proportion of never-selected clients and per-client or worst-client accuracy were not recorded.
\item \textbf{Baselines.} For each baseline it is not stated whether results were reproduced in our implementation or taken from the cited publication under its own architecture and preprocessing. Oort, FedCor, q-FFL/AFL, SCAFFOLD and FedNova are discussed but not evaluated, and there is no controlled head-to-head comparison against AHIBCS, FedFairCS and Zhai et al.\ in one framework.
\item \textbf{Implementation details not recorded.} The ResNet-18 pretraining checkpoint and preprocessing, the values of $\mu$ and $B$, the Configuration~B and~C cohort bounds and per-round cohort logs, the corruption process of S3 and S4, the COVID-19 dataset version, class counts, splits and patient-level grouping, and the averaging convention of recall and F1 were not recorded.
\item \textbf{Scale and setting.} At most 100 simulated clients in a synchronous setting were used. Claims of scalability or deployment are not supported.
\item \textbf{Trust.} Client-reported scores are not verified (Section~\ref{sec4.10}); robustness to adversarial or Byzantine clients is unexplored.
\item \textbf{Reproducibility.} The code and dataset partitions behind the reported numbers are not available. A future package should contain configuration files, partition identifiers, seeds, per-round scores, cohort selections, aggregation weights and metric outputs.
\end{enumerate}

%% ------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec7}
We presented FedFACS, a client selection framework that ranks clients by a fitness score combining a KL-divergence disagreement term, an idle-round fairness term and a batch-variance penalty, adapts the cohort size and utility--fairness mixing from validation-loss feedback, and aggregates selected updates with normalised nonnegative stability weights. On MNIST, FMNIST, CIFAR-10 and COVID-19 Radiography, single-run experiments show fewer rounds than Zhai et al.\ in all Configuration~A settings, higher accuracy and fewer cumulative uploads than AHIBCS in Configuration~B at the cost of more rounds on two of three datasets and lower COVID-19 recall, and a higher Jain index than FedFairCS in five of six noisy-data settings. The results describe trade-offs among accuracy, convergence rounds, uploads and quality-adjusted participation, not simultaneous superiority. They were obtained with an implementation that preceded the corrected formulation and are single-run, so they should be regarded as preliminary evidence. Future work should re-evaluate the corrected specification over multiple seeds with component-isolated ablations, measure total communication and computation, report client-level fairness, test sensitivity and scalability, and address Byzantine and data-poisoning clients.

