277	C_{\mathrm{total}}(T)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}|C_k^t| .
278	\end{equation}
279	For an attempted round $t$, let $D^t$, $U^t$ and $A^t$ be the model-download, update-upload and auxiliary application traffic. Total counted communication is
280	\begin{equation}\label{eq:btotal}
281	B_{\mathrm{total}}(T)=B_{\mathrm{setup}}+B_{\mathrm{cal}}+B_{\mathrm{final}}+\sum_{t\in\mathcal{R}(T)}\bigl(D^t+U^t+A^t\bigr),
282	\end{equation}
283	where $B_{\mathrm{setup}}$ is one-time setup traffic, $B_{\mathrm{cal}}$ is calibration traffic (zero for FedFACS under the causal reference of Eq.~\eqref{eq:omega}, nonzero for any method that needs a prior calibration run), $B_{\mathrm{final}}$ is final-model distribution traffic, and $\mathcal{R}(T)$ is the set of attempted rounds up to $T$, including partial and cancelled attempts. Eq.~\eqref{eq:btotal} defines the accounting; \emph{it was not measured in our experiments}, and cumulative uploads are only one component of it.
284	\emph{Jain's fairness index} \citep{shi2024,cho2020} is
285	\begin{equation}\label{eq:jfi}
286	\mathrm{JFI}=\frac{\bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_i/q_i\bigr)^2}{n\sum_{i=1}^{n}(p_i/q_i)^2},\qquad q_i=(\text{number of labels})\times(1-\text{fraction of noisy data}),
287	\end{equation}
288	where $p_i$ is the participation rate of client $i$ in aggregation, $n$ is the client population and $q_i$ is the quality of its local data \citep{shi2023}. Because it is applied to $p_i/q_i$, $\mathrm{JFI}=1$ means participation proportional to quality, which equals equal raw participation only if all $q_i$ are equal. The minimum possible value is $1/n$, and the threshold of 0.5 sometimes used for description is a convention, not a property of the index. An index between 0.5 and 1 does not imply that every client participates: with equal quality, serving 90 of 100 clients uniformly and never serving the other ten gives $0.9$. Part of any JFI difference may also reflect selection correlating with data quality rather than with participation equality.

