353	Each participating client is denoted by $C_i$, where $i \in \{1,\ldots,C\}$. At round $t$, the central server sends $w_{\text{global}}^t$ to all participating clients $C_i$. The KL divergence represents the client utility introduced previously in Eq. (3). Client $i$ produces the predictive distribution $P(x)$, where the corresponding global-model prediction is denoted by $Q(x)$, as defined in Eq. (5) (Amini et al. 2025). Furthermore, KL divergence is used as an indicator of the divergence between local and global predictive distributions, where larger values reflect a greater potential to improve the global model with new information. Therefore, selecting clients with higher KL divergence reduces the inconsistency between local and global predictive outputs, thereby enhancing model stability and generalization.
354	\begin{equation}
355	\Delta KL(P \parallel Q) = \sum_{x \in X} P(x)\log\left(\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\right)
356	\label{eq:kl}
357	\end{equation}
358	
359	Fairness serves a vital function in preserving global model generalization. Without a fairness component, client selection approaches tend to repeatedly select a limited group of highly informative clients, leading to a biased global model toward particular data distributions while neglecting other less informative clients. The normalized fairness term in the proposed model is defined based on the consecutive idle rounds $r_i^t$ for client $i$, which is not selected up to round $t$. This is inspired by the Age Of Information (AOI) metric (Xu et al., 2024), and the average idle rounds across all participating clients at communication round $t$, represented by $\overline{r^t}$, which the server computes, as shown in Eq.~(6). The normalization is scale-invariant and prevents division by zero during the initial training round or when all clients are selected by the server, while adding 1 to the fraction as a baseline offset to ensure numerical stability. In addition, it evaluates a client’s need for participation relative to the current client activity.
360	\begin{equation}
361	F_i^t = 1 + \frac{r_i^t}{1 + \overline{r^t}}
362	\label{eq:fairness}
363	\end{equation}
364	
365	The counter of idle rounds $r_i^t$ is updated every round according to Eq.~(7), where $C_k^t$ denotes the size of the client cohort selected at round $t$.
366	
367	\begin{equation}
368	r_i^{t+1} =
369	\begin{cases}
370	0 & \text{if } i \in C_k^t  \\
371	r_i^t & \text{otherwise}
372	\end{cases}
373	\label{eq:idle_update}
374	\end{equation}
375	
376	The proposed control mechanism is inspired by the Lyapunov stability principle, where the feedback signal guides the system in determining whether to shift toward informativeness or fairness. At the same time, the use of bounded and normalized updates helps ensure stable behavior during training. The control mechanism is defined as follows:
377	\begin{equation}
378	a_i^t = clip(a,1,0) = \prod_{[0,1]}
379	\left(
380	\beta \cdot a_i^{t-1} + (1-\beta)
381	\frac{
382	\nu^t \cdot (\Delta KL_i^t - F_i^t)
383	}{
384	\lambda \cdot F + \nu^t \cdot (\Delta KL_i^t - F_i^t)
385	}
386	\right)
387	\end{equation}
388	
389	
390	The term $\Delta KL_i^t - F_i^t$ provides a directional signal for decision-making, while incorporating the fairness term in the denominator helps stabilize the update and avoids rapid shifts caused by under-served clients. This leads to a smooth and balanced control mechanism. The parameter $\beta \in [0,1]$ is an exponential moving average smoothing parameter, $\lambda$ is a fairness regularization coefficient, and $\prod_{[0,1]}(\cdot)$ represents the Euclidean projection to ensure that the parameter remains within the bounded interval.
391	
392	The fairness value increases when a client remains unselected, forcing other metrics in the priority score to encourage its selection as in Eq. (9), where the contribution of client $i$ during communication round $t$ is represented by the priority score $p_i^t$. This score combines two main factors such as: fairness and utility while weighting them through a control mechanism by tuning the control parameter $a$, allowing the framework to prioritize fairness or utility depending on the global model’s needs for either exploitation or exploration.
393	\begin{equation}
394	P_i^t = a \cdot \Delta KL_i^t + (1 - a)\, F_i^t
395	\label{eq:priority}
396	\end{equation}
397	
398	By adjusting the control parameter $a_i^t \in [0,1]$, the control mechanism dynamically balances informativeness against fairness. This parameter is updated based on the feedback deviation control signal $\nu^t$, which reflects the current convergence behavior of the global model.
399	
400	The deviation control signal $\nu^t$ is computed on the server side and defined as the difference between the adaptive reference convergence rate $\Omega^t$ and the current global convergence efficiency $\rho^t$, as shown in Eq.~(10). This signal acts as the main feedback mechanism that adaptively adjusts the exploration--exploitation balance at each training round.
401	When $\nu^t > 0$, the convergence is slower than the reference rate, leading to an increase in the control parameter $a_i^t$ to prioritize the selection of informative clients and accelerate convergence. Conversely, when $\nu^t \le 0$, the convergence is faster than the reference rate, leading to a decrease in the control parameter to prioritize fairness and improve client participation.
402	Therefore, the introduction of the deviation control signal is essential for providing the framework with performance-aware behavior.
403	\begin{equation}
404	\nu^t = \Omega^t - \rho^t
405	\label{eq:deviation}
406	\end{equation}
