461	S_i^t = \frac{s_i^t}{\sum_{i \in C_k^t} s_i^t}
462	\label{eq:stability_norm}
463	\end{equation}
464	
465	One more step, the resulting model is assessed on a separate validation set, then the centralized server updates its deviation control signal and global fairness baseline as defined in Eq.~(19). As the final step, the resulting global model parameters $w_{{global}}^{t+1}$, $\overline{r^{t+1}}$, and $\nu^{t+1}$ are broadcast to the entire set of $C$ clients for the following training round.
466	
467	\begin{equation}
468	\overline{r^{t+1}} = \frac{1}{K} \sum_{i=1}^{K} r_i^t
469	\label{eq:avg_idle_correct}
470	\end{equation}
471	
472	The proposed approach introduces a fitness scoring method that ensures the selection of high-quality and and informative clients, while preserving fair participation according to client’s informativeness and the current level of model convergence. This allows the framework to explore clients that may provide useful information during stable improvement, allowing them to contribute. However, it forces the system to exploit the more informative clients during degradation, preventing bias in the global model and preserving balance when system stagnates. As a result, the proposed approach enhances the overall performance by improving accuracy, reducing communication overhead by selecting the more contributing clients to send their updates, and ensuring fair participation. 
473	
474	Furthermore, the aggregation process incorporates stability-based weighting, reflecting the effectiveness and relative contribution across individual client models with respect to its stability score. This approach assigns higher aggregation weights to the informative and stable clients. In addition, it does not compromise fairness but only reduces the implications arising from exploring less utility clients, while assigning lower weights to less informative clients.
