<pasted_content id="3c94">
In federated learning, distributed clients independently optimize their local models using private datasets, while the server coordinates these local updates to develop a unified global model without transferring clients’ original data. This objective is achieved by aggregating participants’ updates through weighted averaging.

Let $C$=\(\{1,\dots,N\}\) be the client population, $D_i$ the dataset of client $i$, $n_i=|D_i|$\textgreater{}0, and $n = \sum_i n_i$, with \(\ell\) the per-example loss and \((x_{ij}, y_{ij})\) the \(j\)-th labeled example of client $i$. As data across clients are non-IID, variations in dataset size, quality, and distribution exist across clients, the learning objective over the local model parameters $w$ and the local empirical loss are

\begin{equation}
F(w) \;=\; \sum_{i = 1}^{N} \frac{n_i}{n} f_i(w)
\label{eq:global_obj}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
f_i(w) = \frac{1}{n_i} \sum_{j=1}^{n_i} \ell(w; x_{ij}, y_{ij})
\label{eq:local_obj}
\end{equation}

In practical federated learning, several challenges arise as data heterogeneity leads to biased, unstable, and noisy updates. Moreover, selecting all clients every training round is impractical due to communication overhead. In addition, unfair client selection leads to repeatedly choosing high-quality clients which biases the global model. All of these issues negatively affect the overall performance.

At round \(t\), the server selects a cohort \(C_k^t \subseteq C\) of size \(m_t\), based on a fitness score that reflects the effectiveness of each client \(i \in C\), as defined in Eq.~(3). The selected cohort aims to maximize the total fitness with ties broken by ascending client consecutive idle rounds, thereby improving global model performance. The terms introduced in Eqs. (3) and (4) are defined and explained in Section 4.2 according to the proposed approach:

\begin{equation}
C_k^t \in \arg\max_{\substack{A \subseteq C \\ |A| = m_t}} \sum_{i \in A} \mathrm{Fitness}_i^t
\label{eq:client_selection}
\end{equation}


To reduce communication overhead, the number of uploaded models is controlled by dynamically adjusting the cohort size based on global convergence efficiency. This size is constrained by \(C_{k_{\min}} \leq C_k^t \leq C_{k_{\max}}\), where \(C_{k_{\min}}\) and \(C_{k_{\max}}\) represent the lower and upper bounds, respectively.

The next global model is the stability-weighted average of the trained local models \(w_i^t\), with nonnegative stability weights \(S_i^t\), that sum to one, as shown in Eq.~(4). This amplifies the contribution of the selected clients to improve overall performance. Subsequently, the resulting aggregated model is utilized in the next iteration.

\begin{equation}
w_{\text{global}}^{t+1} = \sum_{i \in C_k^t} \mathrm{S}_i^t  w_i^t
\label{eq:global_update}
\end{equation}
</pasted_content id="3c94">

